  "label": "Single Cell Perturbation Response Prediction",
  "related": [
    "regulatory-gene-9c82",
    "open-electrophysiological-e8a5",
    "automated-microscopy-2e31"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "ai-topic-c4ec",
  "summary": "New perturbation models arrive as benchmarks show global Pearson inflates performance and drug-induced responses concord poorly across datasets.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is predicting single-cell transcriptional responses to unseen perturbations, now paired with sharper scrutiny of evaluation. On the modeling side, Graph-guided Flow Matching couples knowledge graphs with latent flow matching for unseen Perturb-seq targets. scBIG++ models all genes via gene-module clustering across genetic and chemical perturbations. S1Pert predicts target-specific responses using Welch/equivalence-test labels with a frozen encoder. PertVI disentangles mitotic phase, cell state, batch and treatment, and a normalized HSIC regularizer for concept bottleneck models targets nonlinear dependence between known and unknown factors in counterfactuals. On the evaluation side, the Delta Gap study reports GEARS delta Pearson of 0.076 versus 0.267 for a mean-delta baseline on Replogle K562, arguing that global correlation inflates performance. A gene-similarity analysis links agreement between biological priors and Perturb-seq structure to target-level predictability. A six-resource benchmark finds limited cross-source concordance of drug-induced responses. Adjacent work covers foundation models (LingoCell, Multicellular Transformer) and synthetic bulk RNA-seq benchmarking.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-17"
}
